    title: "Why is Erdogan's son meeting Lebanon’s top leaders?",
    summary: "Why is Erdogan's son meeting Lebanon’s top leaders? Submitted by Ragip Soylu on Fri, 11/13/2026 - 17:38 As Turkey seeks a larger role in Lebanon, Bilal Erdogan’s meetings with political and religious leaders has put his growing international profile in focus Son of Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan and member of the Turkey Youth Foundation High Advisory Board, Bilal Erdogan, at a press conference ahead of a pro-Palestine rally in Istanbul, Turkey, on 22 December 2025 (Yasin Akgul/AFP) Off Necmettin Bilal Erdogan, the son of Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan, has raised eyebrows in the Middle East by meeting with top Lebanese officials over the past two days. Erdogan holds no official role in the Turkish government, but is known for promoting traditional sports, humanitarian aid, and philanthropy worldwide. \"The visit is social and economic in nature,\" a senior Lebanese government official told Middle East Eye. \"Erdogan brought with him Turkish businessmen and institutions, and came to send a message of support to Lebanon.\" According to the official, the visit was coordinated with the Lebanese state. The encounters have prompted questions about the purpose of his trip, as he held high-level meetings in Beirut during a time of heightened tensions in the region. Some in the Middle East regard him as a potential successor to his father. But sources close to Erdogan told MEE that the visit was one of many trips he has made across the region and beyond in recent years, and carries no political significance. Erdogan was in Tokyo earlier this month and also visited Syria earlier this year. He also travels to Europe, North Africa and South Asia on trips he arranges independently. Erdogan made a point of including Selim Argun, president of the Yunus Emre Institute, in his delegation to highlight the cultural focus of his itinerary. The institute promotes the Turkish language, arts and cultural heritage through its branches around the world. Invited by Deputy Prime Minister Tarek Mitri, who oversees Lebanon’s relations with Ankara and Damascus, Erdogan met senior Lebanese officials, including President Joseph Aoun. The visit comes as Turkey seeks a larger role in Lebanon by deepening political ties, helping Syria repair its relations with Beirut and offering technical and economic assistance. Reuters reported last month that Ankara had hosted talks aimed at defusing tensions between Hezbollah and the new Syrian government, which were on opposing sides of the Syrian civil war. Both the Turkish and Syrian governments denied the report. What happened in the meetings? In his meetings, Erdogan discussed joint projects between Turkish companies and institutions and Lebanese state bodies across several sectors, including transport, trade and exports, the official said. \"In every meeting he held, he warned of Israel and the danger it poses to Lebanon and Syria, and stressed Turkey's role in linking the two countries,\" the official added. Another source involved in planning the trip told MEE it was purely aimed at building cultural bridges, with no political ambitions. The source dismissed suggestions that Erdogan might be working behind closed doors to ease regional tensions, including those between Syria and Hezbollah. “It's exploring Lebanon and visiting the key people and hearing from them - and friendship - and understanding the region,” the source said. “So far it's been well received from everyone.” Trablusşam’da izcilerden Bilal Erdoğan’a tekbirli uğurlama. Lübnan ziyaretinin ikinci gününde Trablusşam’a giden Bilal Erdoğan’ı, kentte faaliyet gösteren İman İzcilik Derneği’nin üyeleri Türk ve Lübnan bayraklarıyla uğurladı. pic.twitter.com/ISRGtlqBMc — serbestiyet (@serbestiyetweb) November 13, 2026 Translation: On the second day of his visit to Lebanon, Bilal Erdogan travelled to Tripoli, where members of the city’s Iman Scouting Association, carrying Turkish and Lebanese flags, saw him off. Speaking on Friday at the Lebanon-Turkey Strategic Leadership Dialogue, hosted by the Lebanese economy ministry, Erdogan said his visit sought to reaffirm the deep-rooted ties between the two peoples, highlighting the historical and cultural dimensions of the relationship. He said that, given the right conditions, Lebanon and Syria could experience economic growth similar to Turkey’s over the past 20 years. Stability in nearby countries, including Greece, Armenia, Lebanon and Syria, was in Turkey’s interest and would contribute to regional prosperity and peace, he added. On Thursday, Erdogan held separate meetings with parliament speaker Nabih Berri, Future Movement Vice President Bahia Hariri, and Druze leader Walid Jumblatt, the former head of the Progressive Socialist Party. He travelled to Tripoli on Friday, a Sunni stronghold, where footage showed crowds welcoming him with Turkish flags. According to the Anadolu Agency, Erdogan was received by Tripoli's mufti, Sheikh Muhammad Imam, and the head of the Alawi Islamic Council, Sheikh Ali Qaddour. He also met Muslim and Christian religious leaders in a mosque’s meeting hall. Those attending included the Maronite Archbishop of Tripoli, Youssef Soueif, and Greek Orthodox Archbishop Ephrem Kyriakos, the agency reported. Erdogan told those gathered that Turkey and Lebanon needed to strengthen their fraternal ties, as did Lebanon and Syria, and Turkey and Syria. He said Muslim countries and other nations in the region should prioritise the well-being of their immediate neighbours before looking further afield, stressing the importance of regional solidarity. Inside Lebanon MEE correspondent Beirut Ankara News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
